1
00:00:03,690 --> 00:00:05,020
Hello.

2
00:00:05,430 --> 00:00:07,010
I'm Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

3
00:00:07,010 --> 00:00:10,550
And welcome to Sheldon Cooper Presents: Fun with Flags,

4
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:14,430
the Final Episode Flagtacular.

5
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:18,330
I knew it was coming. Still scary.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,810
Now, I'm sure you're thinking, "The final episode?

7
00:00:21,810 --> 00:00:24,660
who will stand between us and flag ignorance"

8
00:00:25,290 --> 00:00:27,170
I know I was thinking that.

9
00:00:27,670 --> 00:00:31,100
Is this a show on flags or mind reading?

10
00:00:31,710 --> 00:00:34,860
But the truth is I can no longer balance a full-time career,

11
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:37,650
a popular Internet show, and a girlfriend.

12
00:00:37,650 --> 00:00:41,170
And he really does have one, you jerks on the comment board.

13
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:45,930
So as they say, all good things must come to an end.

14
00:00:45,930 --> 00:00:49,130
After only 232 episodes.

15
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:51,410
233 if we include the one

16
00:00:51,410 --> 00:00:54,550
somebody forgot to press record on.

17
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:56,570
You said you weren't gonna bring that up.

18
00:00:56,570 --> 00:00:58,870
And you said you pressed record.

19
00:01:00,690 --> 00:01:03,030
Anyway, please sit back, relax,

20
00:01:03,030 --> 00:01:05,500
and join us as we take our final lap...

21
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:10,230
...as indicated by the waving of this racing flag.

22
00:01:10,230 --> 00:01:12,900
And, of course, white flags can also represent surrender,

23
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:15,930
the Stewards of Gondor, and the Japanese Minamoto clan,

24
00:01:15,930 --> 00:01:18,600
but I'm sure you remember that from episode 62,

25
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:21,880
"White Flags: Who's Wavin' 'Em and Why"

26
00:01:23,180 --> 00:01:24,110
That was a good one.

27
00:01:24,110 --> 00:01:26,050
It was so good.

28
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:55,630
You know, I thought cleaning out a dead professor's office

29
00:01:55,630 --> 00:01:57,290
was gonna just be boring,

30
00:01:57,290 --> 00:01:59,490
but then I found his dentures

31
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:02,750
and realized it's also gross.

32
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:06,970
Doesn't Professor Abbott's family want any of this stuff?

33
00:02:06,970 --> 00:02:08,280
I don't think he had a family.

34
00:02:08,280 --> 00:02:09,910
- That's rough. - I know.

35
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:11,830
You can spend your whole life working,

36
00:02:11,830 --> 00:02:15,500
and at the end all that's left are some papers in a box.

37
00:02:15,830 --> 00:02:17,490
Makes you think.

38
00:02:18,390 --> 00:02:20,230
Oh, well.

39
00:02:20,890 --> 00:02:22,950
Hey. Hey, we're supposed to look through all this stuff

40
00:02:22,950 --> 00:02:24,710
before we throw it away in case it's important.

41
00:02:24,710 --> 00:02:27,430
I-I did. It's all outdated or disproved.

42
00:02:27,430 --> 00:02:31,100
I don't know. This old pen kind of proves gravity.

43
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:34,420
When I tilt it, her bathing suit falls right off.

44
00:02:35,190 --> 00:02:37,370
Oh, my dad used to have a pen like that.

45
00:02:37,370 --> 00:02:40,150
I dated it all through sixth grade.

46
00:02:40,870 --> 00:02:41,690
It still feels weird

47
00:02:41,690 --> 00:02:43,590
just throwing away his work like this.

48
00:02:43,590 --> 00:02:44,740
Don't feel bad.

49
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:47,570
Someday, someone will be throwing out your work, too.

50
00:02:48,870 --> 00:02:52,120
That someone was Sheldon, and the day was yesterday.

51
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:54,760
Hey, look at this.

52
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:59,110
"Dear Roger, to be opened upon your first great discovery.

53
00:02:59,110 --> 00:03:00,750
Love, Mom."

54
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:03,180
Wow, look at the date.

55
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:06,160
He saved this 50 years and never got to open it.

56
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:08,960
That's intense.

57
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:11,050
Poor Roger Abbott.

58
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:16,430
Roger Abbott sounds just like Roger Rabbit.

59
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:20,420
Roger Abbott, Roger Abbott, Roger Rabbit.

60
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:22,080
Oh, my God, Leonard, I know he's dead,

61
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:24,170
but try it. It's fun!

62
00:03:24,170 --> 00:03:26,580
Roger Abbott, Roger Rabbit...

63
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:29,970
Penny!

64
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:32,360
Oh, my rookie of the year.

65
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:35,020
Oh, Dan. My boss who didn't want to hire me

66
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:36,980
and now's a little hug machine.

67
00:03:37,730 --> 00:03:41,550
Oh, look at you. Brand-new and already the third best sales rep.

68
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:43,390
- Why don't you come over to my table. - Yes. I'll be right there.

69
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:45,400
Bernadette's just parking the car.

70
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:47,090
Oh, good.

71
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:50,600
Bernadette. Cute, sweet...

72
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:53,180
vicious little Bernadette.

73
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:55,270
Come on. She's not that bad.

74
00:03:55,270 --> 00:03:56,040
Oh, yeah?

75
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:58,560
At the company picnic she yelled at me and my grandson

76
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:00,850
for losing the three-legged race.

77
00:04:00,850 --> 00:04:05,400
I mean, he still calls her "That mean kid with the big boobies."

78
00:04:06,450 --> 00:04:08,270
I know she can be a little intense, but, I swear,

79
00:04:08,270 --> 00:04:10,350
she is so sweet once you get past all the...

80
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:11,630
Hey, what are you talking about?

81
00:04:11,630 --> 00:04:14,730
We were just talking about how much we love working with you.

82
00:04:14,730 --> 00:04:15,820
Isn't that right?

83
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:17,280
Dan?

84
00:04:20,890 --> 00:04:23,090
Over the years here at Fun With Flags,

85
00:04:23,090 --> 00:04:26,740
we've had an opportunity to learn, laugh, wonder...

86
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:30,330
And, yes, even shed a tear or two.

87
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:34,120
Like when you do a two-hour Fourth of July spectacular

88
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:36,640
and it doesn't get recorded.

89
00:04:37,950 --> 00:04:39,870
How many times do I have to say I'm sorry?

90
00:04:39,870 --> 00:04:41,980
How about 4,000.

91
00:04:43,010 --> 00:04:46,440
One for every domino I set up to make that American flag.

92
00:04:48,030 --> 00:04:50,370
Please enjoy these highlights.

93
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:54,770
Crikey! What flag do we have today?

94
00:04:58,770 --> 00:05:00,920
Australia, mate!

95
00:05:04,450 --> 00:05:08,870
And now it's time for the speed round of Flag or Not a Flag.

96
00:05:08,870 --> 00:05:11,040
Flag. Flag.

97
00:05:11,450 --> 00:05:13,520
Not a flag. Flag.

98
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:15,560
I am crushing this.

99
00:05:20,710 --> 00:05:24,420
Say, Betsy Ross, what you working on?

100
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:26,980
I have no idea.

101
00:05:27,950 --> 00:05:31,510
Because the story of me sewing the first American flag

102
00:05:31,510 --> 00:05:34,210
is unsupported poppycock.

103
00:05:35,010 --> 00:05:36,940
Then who did sew it, hmm?

104
00:05:36,940 --> 00:05:38,690
Don't ask me.

105
00:05:38,690 --> 00:05:42,350
I'm just a simple seamstress whose descendants are...

106
00:05:42,350 --> 00:05:44,700
out to make a quick buck!

107
00:05:52,370 --> 00:05:54,210
Fancy a dip, my dear?

108
00:05:54,210 --> 00:05:55,790
I do.

109
00:05:58,220 --> 00:06:00,390
Wait, that purple flag indicates

110
00:06:00,390 --> 00:06:03,380
that marine pests, such as jellyfish, are present.

111
00:06:03,380 --> 00:06:06,570
Wow, that flag is a lifesaver.

112
00:06:06,570 --> 00:06:07,710
No.

113
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:10,790
This is.

114
00:06:12,510 --> 00:06:14,500
Stop looking at my legs.

115
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:17,830
Not a flag. Flag.

116
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:20,620
Flag. Not a flag.

117
00:06:21,070 --> 00:06:21,950
Flag.

118
00:06:22,440 --> 00:06:23,530
Crap.

119
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:27,500
When stuff like this gets me down,

120
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:28,920
you know what I like to do?

121
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:32,140
Sing "Hakuna Matata" Like an eight-year-old girl?

122
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:33,940
Wrong, smarty-pants.

123
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:37,110
It's "Everything Is Awesome" From The Lego Movie.

124
00:06:39,060 --> 00:06:40,200
Look at these notebooks.

125
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:42,350
They're full of pages and pages of data.

126
00:06:42,350 --> 00:06:44,230
He clearly spent years on this.

127
00:06:44,230 --> 00:06:46,960
Oh, it's just columns of random numbers. Toss it.

128
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:49,900
Well, but what if this was a potential breakthrough for him?

129
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:51,400
What if... what if this is the thing

130
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:53,960
that would've finally let him open that bottle of champagne?

131
00:06:53,960 --> 00:06:56,580
Maybe he didn't know what to pair champagne with.

132
00:06:56,580 --> 00:07:00,370
Strawberries, caviar, oysters. All good choices.

133
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:02,800
Yeah, that look right there

134
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:05,800
is why people eat before they come to your parties.

135
00:07:06,780 --> 00:07:08,970
This has to mean something.

136
00:07:09,610 --> 00:07:12,740
Well, there's no discernible pattern that I can see,

137
00:07:12,740 --> 00:07:15,390
but it shouldn't be too hard to run the numbers and check.

138
00:07:15,390 --> 00:07:17,740
Well, maybe we can get some time on the supercomputer.

139
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:19,560
Sure. We could try multiple regressions

140
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:22,080
with varying physical and experimental constants.

141
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:24,360
Oh, we could run a pattern-matching algorithm

142
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:26,630
against well-known unsolved physics problems.

143
00:07:26,630 --> 00:07:29,590
Ooh, it might be a substitution cipher.

144
00:07:29,590 --> 00:07:31,320
We could start with basic cryptanalysis.

145
00:07:31,320 --> 00:07:34,090
Or we could talk to this guy he used to share an office with.

146
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:38,040
Oh, let's do that.

147
00:07:39,290 --> 00:07:41,540
Because you don't know how to do cryptanalysis, do you?

148
00:07:41,540 --> 00:07:44,060
I'm not even sure if it's the right word.

149
00:07:52,590 --> 00:07:54,860
So, Dan, you have a grandson.

150
00:07:54,860 --> 00:07:56,420
- How old is he? - Seven.

151
00:07:56,420 --> 00:07:59,220
Oh, yeah. I remember him from the picnic.

152
00:07:59,970 --> 00:08:02,600
He was the one crying like a wuss the whole time.

153
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,030
I heard he was being picked on by a mean kid.

154
00:08:07,030 --> 00:08:08,880
Just builds character.

155
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:10,790
Like my dad said, "Nobody likes a crybaby

156
00:08:10,790 --> 00:08:13,070
except their mommies and Democrats."

157
00:08:14,420 --> 00:08:16,280
I need another drink.

158
00:08:17,870 --> 00:08:18,960
What's his problem?

159
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:21,040
I don't know. Maybe he didn't like the fact

160
00:08:21,040 --> 00:08:22,920
that you called his grandson a wuss.

161
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:25,940
Well, I didn't think you were supposed to say "sissy" anymore.

162
00:08:27,320 --> 00:08:29,740
Okay, look, I know what a good person you are,

163
00:08:29,740 --> 00:08:32,970
but sometimes people think you might come off a little harsh.

164
00:08:32,970 --> 00:08:34,100
What?

165
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:37,010
I'm, like, the sweetest person I know.

166
00:08:37,730 --> 00:08:41,470
Look at me. I should be in a tree baking cookies.

167
00:08:42,290 --> 00:08:44,270
Yes, but once in a while,

168
00:08:44,270 --> 00:08:47,200
people think you're a little mean.

169
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:48,350
Oh, yeah?

170
00:08:48,350 --> 00:08:50,290
You one of those people?

171
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:53,400
No. No, no, no.

172
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:55,380
I-I think the cookie thing.

173
00:08:59,900 --> 00:09:01,440
Action.

174
00:09:01,440 --> 00:09:03,100
Welcome back.

175
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:06,040
Our guest today is a returning fan favorite.

176
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:08,140
He puts the reading in your rainbow,

177
00:09:08,140 --> 00:09:09,780
the Geordi in your La Forge,

178
00:09:09,780 --> 00:09:11,630
and the Kunta in your Kinte,

179
00:09:11,630 --> 00:09:13,640
Mr. LeVar Burton.

180
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:20,990
Thank you, Sheldon. Now, remember our deal...

181
00:09:20,990 --> 00:09:24,870
You do this, I delete your contact information.

182
00:09:25,340 --> 00:09:26,380
While...

183
00:09:26,380 --> 00:09:28,540
While you watch me do it.

184
00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:30,220
Great. Happy to be back.

185
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:31,760
Well, since you're here,

186
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:34,290
I'd like to get your opinion on something.

187
00:09:34,290 --> 00:09:36,340
In honor of Black History Month,

188
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:38,580
I portrayed George Washington Carver

189
00:09:38,580 --> 00:09:44,080
in a loving tribute that my roommate called "wildly racist"

190
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:45,250
What do you think?

191
00:09:45,250 --> 00:09:47,850
Hi. My name is George.

192
00:09:47,850 --> 00:09:50,080
Oh, hell, no!

193
00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:53,980
You heard him, Leonard.

194
00:09:53,980 --> 00:09:56,900
No, it's not racist.

195
00:10:02,390 --> 00:10:03,800
Are we sure this is the place?

196
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:05,820
The doorman said this is the right building.

197
00:10:05,820 --> 00:10:07,300
I think if you're pulling up your pants,

198
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:09,240
you're not a doorman.

199
00:10:10,710 --> 00:10:11,610
Who is it?

200
00:10:11,610 --> 00:10:13,820
Uh, I'm looking for a Professor Sharpe?

201
00:10:13,820 --> 00:10:15,030
Hold on.

202
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:19,190
- Can I help you? - Hi. We're from the university.

203
00:10:19,190 --> 00:10:21,570
We're trying to get some information about Professor Abbott.

204
00:10:21,570 --> 00:10:22,720
We were cleaning out his office

205
00:10:22,720 --> 00:10:24,940
and found these books full of numbers.

206
00:10:24,940 --> 00:10:26,290
Any chance you know what it is?

207
00:10:26,290 --> 00:10:28,190
Yes, he was always working on this.

208
00:10:28,190 --> 00:10:31,300
I was thinking it might be some sort of interstellar coordinate system,

209
00:10:31,300 --> 00:10:33,360
maybe an updated version of Hubble's law.

210
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:35,820
I keep seeing the number 90 repeating.

211
00:10:35,820 --> 00:10:38,780
That could be the angle of perturbation of a distant galaxy.

212
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:41,240
It's the number of calories in a yogurt.

213
00:10:42,470 --> 00:10:44,060
That's his food diary.

214
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:45,100
Really?

215
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:46,710
Yeah, he wrote down everything he ever ate.

216
00:10:46,710 --> 00:10:49,000
He was convinced that calorie restriction

217
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,570
was the key to living forever.

218
00:10:50,570 --> 00:10:52,240
Does it work?

219
00:10:52,820 --> 00:10:53,750
Seriously?

220
00:10:53,750 --> 00:10:56,810
Well, he could have been hit by a bus. You don't know.

221
00:10:58,370 --> 00:10:59,940
Sorry it's not more interesting.

222
00:10:59,940 --> 00:11:01,540
On the bright side, you didn't have to listen

223
00:11:01,540 --> 00:11:04,220
to his stomach growl for 35 years.

224
00:11:04,220 --> 00:11:06,710
So, was there anything that Professor Abbott worked on

225
00:11:06,710 --> 00:11:10,100
during his life that came close to an accomplishment?

226
00:11:10,100 --> 00:11:14,370
To be honest, his research never amounted to anything.

227
00:11:14,370 --> 00:11:15,280
Oh. You were his colleague.

228
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:16,440
How did your research turn out?

229
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:17,190
Great.

230
00:11:17,190 --> 00:11:20,740
Hey, this is the apartment you get when you win a Nobel.

231
00:11:22,330 --> 00:11:23,940
Well, you could be very frugal.

232
00:11:23,940 --> 00:11:26,760
I'm getting a little tired of everybody's sarcasm.

233
00:11:30,370 --> 00:11:31,810
I'm not a bully.

234
00:11:31,810 --> 00:11:33,660
I mean, maybe I come off a little strong,

235
00:11:33,660 --> 00:11:35,670
but that's only because my dad raised me to be tough

236
00:11:35,670 --> 00:11:37,340
and not to take crap from anybody.

237
00:11:37,340 --> 00:11:38,930
No. That's fine,

238
00:11:38,930 --> 00:11:40,510
but there's a difference between being tough

239
00:11:40,510 --> 00:11:42,250
and telling your friend her new pants

240
00:11:42,250 --> 00:11:44,560
look like a saggy diaper.

241
00:11:46,870 --> 00:11:48,350
I did say that, didn't I?

242
00:11:48,350 --> 00:11:49,220
Yeah, you did.

243
00:11:49,220 --> 00:11:51,930
I felt so self-conscious, I had to return them.

244
00:11:52,920 --> 00:11:54,820
Where? To Babies R Us?

245
00:11:55,950 --> 00:11:57,740
You're doing it again!

246
00:11:57,740 --> 00:11:59,370
Okay, sorry. Can we just go?

247
00:11:59,370 --> 00:12:00,760
I feel like everybody hates me.

248
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:02,300
Oh, come on, they don't hate you, all right?

249
00:12:02,300 --> 00:12:04,140
They're just a little intimidated.

250
00:12:04,140 --> 00:12:05,230
All you need to do is show everyone

251
00:12:05,230 --> 00:12:06,780
how sweet you really are.

252
00:12:06,780 --> 00:12:08,530
- I think I'd rather go. - No, come on.

253
00:12:08,530 --> 00:12:09,830
You are not going anywhere, all right?

254
00:12:09,830 --> 00:12:13,210
I am here for you, and we are gonna fix this together.

255
00:12:13,210 --> 00:12:13,940
Thank you.

256
00:12:13,940 --> 00:12:16,060
Even though your dress is ugly.

257
00:12:19,630 --> 00:12:22,010
Well, my little Flag-keteers,

258
00:12:22,010 --> 00:12:25,930
it looks like the last episode of Fun with Flags is at an end.

259
00:12:26,330 --> 00:12:27,850
If I could,

260
00:12:27,850 --> 00:12:31,630
I would run each and every one of you viewers up a flagpole

261
00:12:31,630 --> 00:12:33,520
and salute you.

262
00:12:34,470 --> 00:12:38,140
And if you touched the ground, burn you.

263
00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:39,440
I'd like to take a moment

264
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:42,010
to personally thank Dr. Amy Farrah Fowler,

265
00:12:42,010 --> 00:12:43,970
who you may or may not know

266
00:12:43,970 --> 00:12:47,140
is the first woman to co-host a flag

267
00:12:47,140 --> 00:12:50,930
or banner-related Internet info-tainment show.

268
00:12:51,850 --> 00:12:54,630
Take that, glass ceiling.

269
00:12:55,510 --> 00:12:58,390
And if I may get serious for a moment,

270
00:12:59,970 --> 00:13:03,220
hosting this show has been...

271
00:13:03,220 --> 00:13:06,030
one crazy ride.

272
00:13:06,830 --> 00:13:09,560
With all its ups and downs,

273
00:13:09,560 --> 00:13:12,400
I wouldn't give it up for the world.

274
00:13:13,270 --> 00:13:15,710
Except for now, when I'm giving it up.

275
00:13:18,970 --> 00:13:21,620
Before I sign off, I'd, uh...

276
00:13:21,940 --> 00:13:25,920
I'd like to share with you all one last use for a white flag.

277
00:13:27,180 --> 00:13:29,460
It's good for times like this.

278
00:13:32,450 --> 00:13:33,880
Good night.

279
00:13:34,870 --> 00:13:36,040
Cut.

280
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:39,310
Sheldon...

281
00:13:41,190 --> 00:13:42,420
that was beautiful.

282
00:13:42,420 --> 00:13:43,540
If you didn't press record...

283
00:13:43,540 --> 00:13:45,860
I pressed it!

284
00:13:50,730 --> 00:13:53,160
Well, that's it.

285
00:13:53,160 --> 00:13:55,950
That's the end of Roger Abbott.

286
00:13:58,350 --> 00:14:01,310
And we still don't know who framed him.

287
00:14:03,570 --> 00:14:05,450
I still keep thinking about how an entire life

288
00:14:05,450 --> 00:14:08,000
can seemingly amount to nothing.

289
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,990
I guess the sad truth is,

290
00:14:10,990 --> 00:14:14,150
not everyone will accomplish something great.

291
00:14:14,540 --> 00:14:16,970
Some of us may just have to find meaning

292
00:14:16,970 --> 00:14:21,340
in the little moments that make up life.

293
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:24,150
That's a nice way of looking at it.

294
00:14:24,660 --> 00:14:25,640
Yeah for you, not for me.

295
00:14:25,640 --> 00:14:27,920
I went to space. I'm covered.

296
00:14:30,890 --> 00:14:32,030
You know what?

297
00:14:32,030 --> 00:14:35,090
This bottle was meant to celebrate an achievement.

298
00:14:35,090 --> 00:14:36,460
Let's make a pact.

299
00:14:36,460 --> 00:14:38,780
When one of us gets their first big breakthrough,

300
00:14:38,780 --> 00:14:41,570
we'll celebrate by opening this bottle of champagne

301
00:14:41,570 --> 00:14:43,900
and toasting Professor Abbott.

302
00:14:43,900 --> 00:14:44,630
I love that.

303
00:14:44,630 --> 00:14:46,270
Yeah, me, too.

304
00:14:46,270 --> 00:14:47,710
Then, of course,

305
00:14:47,710 --> 00:14:49,540
rubbing our success in Sheldon's face.

306
00:14:49,540 --> 00:14:51,200
- Oh, well, that's the best part. - Oh, yeah.

307
00:14:53,560 --> 00:14:55,940
Dan, could I talk to you for a minute?

308
00:14:59,630 --> 00:15:00,760
Sure, go ahead.

309
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:03,220
Just want you to know I didn't mean

310
00:15:03,220 --> 00:15:04,720
to be rude about your grandson.

311
00:15:04,720 --> 00:15:06,300
No, it's okay.

312
00:15:06,300 --> 00:15:07,960
No, it's not.

313
00:15:07,960 --> 00:15:10,050
Penny told me that everyone's scared of me.

314
00:15:10,790 --> 00:15:12,730
What? What?

315
00:15:12,730 --> 00:15:14,080
Why would she say that?

316
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:16,290
- You know she drinks, right? - What?

317
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:19,790
No, she's right, and I just want you to know

318
00:15:19,790 --> 00:15:21,950
that from now on, I'm gonna be much more sensitive.

319
00:15:21,950 --> 00:15:24,410
I don't want anyone walking on eggshells around me.

320
00:15:24,410 --> 00:15:26,550
Oh, well, okay, then, uh,

321
00:15:26,550 --> 00:15:28,650
one thing I've been meaning to tell you

322
00:15:28,650 --> 00:15:31,620
is that the company is gonna stop paying for our coffee.

323
00:15:31,620 --> 00:15:33,490
No problem. When does that start?

324
00:15:33,490 --> 00:15:35,080
Five months ago.

325
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:36,930
- What?! - Yeah.

326
00:15:36,930 --> 00:15:38,560
Who's been paying for my coffee?

327
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:39,610
All of us.

328
00:15:39,610 --> 00:15:41,150
Yeah. It comes from the swear jar

329
00:15:41,150 --> 00:15:43,510
we put money in when you curse.

330
00:15:46,470 --> 00:15:48,140
What else don't I know about?

331
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:51,610
- Uh, well, you know your private bathroom? - Yeah?

332
00:15:51,610 --> 00:15:53,880
That was supposed to be for the whole floor.

333
00:15:53,880 --> 00:15:56,000
- You're kidding. - No, no. It's okay.

334
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,120
It only really affected wheelchair Kathy,

335
00:15:58,120 --> 00:16:00,560
and she's back in the hospital now,

336
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:02,160
so it all worked out.

337
00:16:03,730 --> 00:16:05,280
I feel so bad.

338
00:16:05,280 --> 00:16:08,410
Well, then, maybe you shouldn't have named her "Wheelchair Kathy."

339
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:11,720
Oh, my God!

340
00:16:11,720 --> 00:16:14,960
I thought everybody liked me, but I'm just a monster.

341
00:16:14,960 --> 00:16:18,910
But a cute one, like that, uh, eyeball guy in Monsters, Inc.

342
00:16:19,830 --> 00:16:21,580
No, I'm the worst.

343
00:16:21,580 --> 00:16:23,410
I'm a terrible person.

344
00:16:23,410 --> 00:16:25,880
Come on, don't cry. It's okay.

345
00:16:25,880 --> 00:16:27,110
It's not okay.

346
00:16:27,110 --> 00:16:29,780
How can I even work here anymore? Oh.

347
00:16:29,780 --> 00:16:31,420
- Because this company needs you. - It's fine.

348
00:16:31,420 --> 00:16:33,150
We don't mind paying for the coffee.

349
00:16:33,150 --> 00:16:35,550
I can't let you do that.

350
00:16:35,560 --> 00:16:37,190
Maybe we could get you

351
00:16:37,190 --> 00:16:39,670
an espresso machine for your office.

352
00:16:39,950 --> 00:16:43,390
- I guess that would be all right. - All right.

353
00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:47,620
See? You don't have to be mean to get what you want.

354
00:16:47,620 --> 00:16:49,310
You're right.

355
00:16:49,310 --> 00:16:52,740
Now where did we land on my bathroom?

356
00:16:57,540 --> 00:16:58,390
Hi.

357
00:16:58,650 --> 00:16:59,520
Hello.

358
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:01,210
How come you're up so late?

359
00:17:01,210 --> 00:17:04,610
I posted the last episode of Fun with Flags hours ago,

360
00:17:04,610 --> 00:17:08,140
and not a single person cared enough to comment.

361
00:17:09,570 --> 00:17:11,550
All that effort for nothing.

362
00:17:12,750 --> 00:17:14,130
I know how you feel.

363
00:17:14,130 --> 00:17:16,360
I spent the day throwing out a man's entire career,

364
00:17:16,360 --> 00:17:18,300
and all that's left is an old bottle of champagne

365
00:17:18,300 --> 00:17:20,660
and a naked lady pen that Raj took

366
00:17:20,660 --> 00:17:22,690
when he thought no one was looking.

367
00:17:24,870 --> 00:17:26,620
It's not the same thing.

368
00:17:26,620 --> 00:17:29,040
I don't think you know how I feel at all.

369
00:17:29,040 --> 00:17:31,070
Sad?

370
00:17:32,970 --> 00:17:34,900
You do get me.

371
00:17:37,970 --> 00:17:39,340
Someone left a comment.

372
00:17:39,340 --> 00:17:40,620
Yeah. What did they say?

373
00:17:40,620 --> 00:17:42,580
"Too bad your show is done.

374
00:17:42,580 --> 00:17:44,190
I kind of liked it."

375
00:17:44,190 --> 00:17:47,140
Leonard, did you hear that?

376
00:17:48,030 --> 00:17:50,870
Oh, the people are heartbroken!

377
00:17:51,790 --> 00:17:54,720
I can't take this away from the world!

378
00:17:54,720 --> 00:17:57,550
Fun with Flags is back!

379
00:17:58,860 --> 00:18:00,010
Congratulations.

380
00:18:00,010 --> 00:18:01,740
Let's celebrate!

381
00:18:04,820 --> 00:18:07,040
Sheldon, that wasn't for you!

382
00:18:07,040 --> 00:18:08,550
Oh, no, I'm not going to drink it.

383
00:18:08,550 --> 00:18:10,890
I just wanted to hear the pop.

384
00:18:11,860 --> 00:18:14,600
Yeah, I knew it was coming. Still scary.

385
00:18:22,510 --> 00:18:23,640
LeVar Burton?

386
00:18:24,160 --> 00:18:25,370
LeVar Burton!

387
00:18:25,920 --> 00:18:27,310
LeVar Burton!

388
00:18:29,680 --> 00:18:31,230
What are you doing here?

389
00:18:31,230 --> 00:18:33,040
You told me not to call,

390
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:35,220
and I didn't know how else to give you the good news.

391
00:18:35,220 --> 00:18:36,790
Fun with Flags is back,

392
00:18:36,790 --> 00:18:38,790
and you can be in the next episode.

393
00:18:38,790 --> 00:18:40,110
Wil Wheaton said, get a gate.

394
00:18:40,110 --> 00:18:42,000
- I don't know why I didn't get a gate. - You...

395
00:18:43,530 --> 00:18:45,270
At least listen to the premise.

396
00:18:45,270 --> 00:18:47,240
Since you were born in Germany,

397
00:18:47,240 --> 00:18:50,020
I thought we could talk about German flags.

398
00:18:50,410 --> 00:18:54,520
How do you feel about dressing up like a swastika?

